## <a id="openapi"></a>OpenAPI

### <a id="getting-started"></a>Getting started

Enable the new `openapi` feature and add [schemars](https://docs.rs/schemars), which describes the data in your request and response bodies:

```sh
cargo add topcoat --features openapi
cargo add schemars
```

Attach an `Operation` to a route with `with(...)`. Topcoat takes the path, HTTP methods and path parameters from the router, and you add the rest:

```rust
use schemars::JsonSchema;
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use topcoat::{
    Result,
    context::Cx,
    openapi::Operation,
    router::{StatusCode, content::Json, route},
};

#[derive(Deserialize, JsonSchema)]
struct CreateUser {
    /// The name to show for the user.
    name: String,
}

#[derive(Serialize, JsonSchema)]
struct User {
    id: u64,
    name: String,
}

#[route(POST "/api/users", with(
    Operation::new()
        .summary("Create a user")
        .request::<Json<CreateUser>>()
        .response_with::<Json<User>>(StatusCode::CREATED, "The new user.")
        .response(StatusCode::CONFLICT, "The name is taken.")
))]
async fn create_user(cx: &Cx, Json(input): Json<CreateUser>) -> Result<(StatusCode, Json<User>)> {
    let user = User { id: 1, name: input.name };
    Ok((StatusCode::CREATED, Json(user)))
}
```

Doc comments on your types and fields become descriptions in the generated schema.

Use `OpenApi` to set the title, version and other document settings, then pass it your router. Return the `Document` from a route to serve it as JSON:

```rust
use topcoat::openapi::{Document, OpenApi};

#[route(GET "/openapi.json")]
async fn spec(cx: &Cx) -> Result<Document> {
    Ok(OpenApi::new("Acme API", "1.0.0")
        .description("Everything you need for tea.")
        .server("https://api.example.com")
        .generate(&router(cx)))
}
```

You can also generate the document outside a request, for example in a test or a command that writes `openapi.json` with `Document::to_json_pretty`.

### <a id="inherited-settings"></a>Inherited settings

An `Operation` on a layer sets defaults for every route under its path. Each of those routes is included in the document and inherits the layer's tags, responses and other settings. A route can override or extend them with its own operation, or leave the document with `.hidden(true)`:

```rust
#[layer("/api", with(
    Operation::new()
        .tag("api")
        .response(StatusCode::UNAUTHORIZED, "The token is missing or wrong.")
))]
async fn api(cx: &Cx, body: Body, next: Next<'_>) -> Result<Response> {
    next.run(cx, body).await
}

// Documented through the layer, with its tag and 401 response.
#[route(GET "/api/health")]
async fn health(cx: &Cx) -> Result<&'static str> {
    Ok("ok")
}

// Left out of the document.
#[route(GET "/api/debug", with(Operation::new().hidden(true)))]
async fn debug(cx: &Cx) -> Result<&'static str> {
    Ok("debug")
}
```

Operations follow the same rules as route context, so you can also put one on a layout or on the router builder.

### <a id="parameters-bodies-and-authentication"></a>Parameters, bodies and authentication

- `Parameter` describes path, query, header and cookie parameters. Path parameters are included automatically as strings; add a `Parameter` to give one a type or a description.
- `Operation::query::<T>()` documents every field of a `#[query_params]` struct that derives `JsonSchema`.
- JSON and form extractors describe their bodies when their data type implements `JsonSchema`. Implement the `Content` trait to describe a custom body type.
